# State Management — Provider

## 1. What is State Management?

**State** means data that can change while the app is running.
If all these screens need the same cart data, passing it manually between screens becomes difficult.

Examples:

```text
Login status
Cart items
Username
Selected currency
Dark mode
Counter value
API data
Loading status
```

For small screens, `setState()` is enough.

But imagine:

```text
Home Screen
     ↓
Product Screen
     ↓
Cart Screen
     ↓
Checkout Screen
```

If all these screens need the same cart data, passing it manually between screens becomes difficult.

This is where **state management** helps.

---

# 2. `setState()` vs State Management

### Small app

```dart
setState(() {
  count++;
});
```

Good for local screen state.

### Larger app

```text
Many widgets
     ↓
Same data needed
     ↓
Shared state
     ↓
State management
```

Provider is one commonly used approach.

---

# 3. What is Provider?

**Provider** allows widgets to access shared data without manually passing that data through every widget.

Think of it as a **shared data source**.

```text
             Provider
                ↓
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       ↓        ↓        ↓
     Home     Cart    Checkout
```

All three screens can access the same state.

---

# 4. Install Provider

Add the `provider` package to your project.

Then import:

```dart
import 'package:provider/provider.dart';
```

---

# 5. Create a Model

Suppose we have a counter.

```dart
class CounterProvider extends ChangeNotifier {

  int count = 0;

  void increment() {
    count++;
    notifyListeners();
  }

}
```

### Important parts

```dart
ChangeNotifier
```

Allows the class to notify widgets when data changes.

```dart
count
```

Our shared state.

```dart
notifyListeners();
```

Tells listening widgets:

> "The data changed. Update the UI."

---

# 6. Provide the State

Put the provider above the widgets that need it.

```dart
void main() {
  runApp(
    ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (_) => CounterProvider(),
      child: MyApp(),
    ),
  );
}
```

Now `CounterProvider` is available to the app below it.

---

# 7. Read the Data

Inside a widget:

```dart
final counter = Provider.of<CounterProvider>(context);
```

Then:

```dart
Text(
  "${counter.count}",
)
```

---

# 8. Change the Data

Call the provider method:

```dart
counter.increment();
```

Complete button:

```dart
ElevatedButton(
  onPressed: () {
    counter.increment();
  },
  child: Text("Add"),
)
```

When `increment()` runs:

```text
count++
   ↓
notifyListeners()
   ↓
Listening widgets rebuild
   ↓
New count appears
```

---

# 9. Complete Example

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:provider/provider.dart';

class CounterProvider extends ChangeNotifier {

  int count = 0;

  void increment() {
    count++;
    notifyListeners();
  }

}

void main() {
  runApp(
    ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (_) => CounterProvider(),
      child: MyApp(),
    ),
  );
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {

  const MyApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: CounterScreen(),
    );
  }
}

class CounterScreen extends StatelessWidget {

  const CounterScreen({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {

    final counter = Provider.of<CounterProvider>(context);

    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text("Counter"),
      ),

      body: Center(
        child: Text(
          "${counter.count}",
          style: TextStyle(fontSize: 40),
        ),
      ),

      floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(
        onPressed: () {
          counter.increment();
        },
        child: Icon(Icons.add),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

---

# 10. `notifyListeners()` is Important

Suppose:

```dart
void increment() {
  count++;
}
```

The value changes, but Provider does not know that widgets need updating.

So we use:

```dart
notifyListeners();
```

Therefore:

```dart
void increment() {
  count++;
  notifyListeners();
}
```

Memory:

```text
Change data
    ↓
notifyListeners()
    ↓
UI updates
```

---

# 11. `Consumer`

Instead of:

```dart
Provider.of<CounterProvider>(context);
```

you can use:

```dart
Consumer<CounterProvider>(
  builder: (context, counter, child) {
    return Text(
      "${counter.count}",
    );
  },
)
```

The `Consumer` listens to the provider and rebuilds when its data changes.

---

# 12. `listen: false`

Sometimes you only want to call a function and don't need the widget to rebuild.

Example:

```dart
final counter = Provider.of<CounterProvider>(
  context,
  listen: false,
);
```

Then:

```dart
counter.increment();
```

This is useful when accessing a provider only for an action.

---

# 13. `setState()` vs Provider

| `setState()`                  | Provider                      |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Local state                   | Shared state                  |
| Simple screens                | Larger apps                   |
| Easy to learn                 | Better for shared data        |
| Usually inside StatefulWidget | Can work with StatelessWidget |
| Small projects                | Multi-screen applications     |

Example:

```text
Password visibility
        ↓
setState()
```

But:

```text
Shopping cart
     ↓
Home
Product
Cart
Checkout
     ↓
Provider
```

---

# 14. Real-World Example — Shopping Cart

Imagine:

```dart
class CartProvider extends ChangeNotifier {

  List<String> cart = [];

  void addToCart(String product) {
    cart.add(product);
    notifyListeners();
  }

  void removeFromCart(String product) {
    cart.remove(product);
    notifyListeners();
  }
}
```

Now:

```text
Product Screen
      ↓
addToCart()
      ↓
CartProvider
      ↓
notifyListeners()
      ↓
Cart Screen updates
```

You don't need to pass the entire cart manually from Product → Home → Cart.

---

# 15. When Should You Use It?

Use `setState()` when:

```text
One screen
Small amount of state
Simple UI
```

Use state management when:

```text
Multiple screens
Shared data
Large application
Complex state
API data used by many widgets
Shopping cart
Authentication
User preferences
```

